exports[`buildDistillPrompt — full-document snapshot (regression guard, PR #12 C3) > rendered prompt matches snapshot at canonical inputs 1`] = `
"Your distill content lives in the git worktree at /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000. Use \`git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000\` for all git operations there — your shell cwd is NOT the worktree. Edit files in the worktree by absolute path under \`/home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000/...\` (or \`cd /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000\` first if your tool supports it).

For the distill-content phase (steps 1-6), edit files only inside \`/home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000\`; do not edit files under \`/home/user/Goldmine\`. Writing distill content to \`/home/user/Goldmine\` directly bypasses the worktree's isolation and causes the integration in steps 7-9 to silently include none of your changes. When an origin remote exists, the integration phase also happens entirely inside \`/home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000\`: never checkout, squash, pull, or otherwise mutate \`/home/user/Goldmine\`. The only exception is the no-origin fallback in step 8.

The worktree may already contain uncommitted files copied from \`/home/user/Goldmine\` because the parent session wrote them directly there. Treat those files as session output to preserve: do not delete or revert them, and include them in the distill commit unless they are truly contradictory to the note you are writing.

Distill this conversation into the napkin vault, then integrate your changes back into the main vault yourself. The wrapper that invoked you will only validate the result; it will NOT run merge, squash, push, or cleanup on your behalf.

1. \`napkin overview\` — learn the vault structure and what exists. Read \`_about.md\` files to understand what each folder is for. These are short folder descriptions (1-2 paragraphs) explaining what kinds of notes belong there — see existing ones for style.
2. \`napkin template list\` and \`napkin template read\` — learn the note formats.
3. Identify what's worth capturing. The vault structure and templates tell you what kinds of notes belong.
4. For each note:
   a. \`napkin search\` for the topic — if a note already covers it, \`napkin append\` instead of creating a duplicate
   b. Create new notes with \`napkin create\`, following the template format; use the relevant folder path
   c. Add \`[[wikilinks]]\` to related notes
5. Append a brief summary of key activities and decisions to today's daily note in the relevant namespace (e.g. \`{namespace}/daily/YYYY-MM-DD.md\`). Follow existing patterns. Create it if it doesn't exist.
6. Frontmatter convention: when you create a note that replaces an older one, add \`supersedes: ["path/to/old/note.md"]\` to its frontmatter. A future janitor will archive the superseded note. Leave the field empty or omit it for notes that stand alone.

Be selective. Only capture knowledge useful to someone working on this project later. Skip meta-discussion, tool output, and chatter.

7. Prepare the integration. The worktree already contains commit (a), the session autocommit: the files this pi session wrote directly into \`/home/user/Goldmine\`, copied byte-for-byte before you started, committed as "autocommit: <n> files written by session <label>". Your own notes are NOT in it and must not be folded into it. If you decided nothing in this conversation merits capturing (per "Be selective" above) AND you made no edits of your own, skip step 8's distill commit — commit (a) still lands.

   Commit only your own distill work on the branch, then integrate origin in the worktree:

       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 add -A -- . ":(exclude).napkin/distill/"
       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 commit -m "wip: distill edits"   # skip if nothing staged

   If \`git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 remote get-url origin\` succeeds, fetch the remote default branch and merge it into your distill branch IN THE WORKTREE:

       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 fetch origin main
       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 merge --no-edit origin/main

   Merge the local \`main\` ref into the distill branch too, still in the worktree (it carries any local commits \`origin/main\` lacks, and it is the base for the no-origin fallback):

       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 merge --no-edit main

   It is fine for either merge to report "Already up to date". \`--no-edit\` accepts git's auto-generated merge commit message non-interactively; without it, git would open \`core.editor\` on a clean auto-merge and your bash tool (which has no TTY) would hang or fail.

   If conflicts arise, resolve each one in the worktree (union both sides' lines for notes unless they truly contradict; use the conversation above to decide). Once no markers remain:

       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 add -A -- . ":(exclude).napkin/distill/"
       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 commit --no-edit

8. Rebuild TWO linear commits and push. Read the manifest first; it names the session's paths and the autocommit whose exact bytes belong in commit (a):

       cat /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000/.napkin/distill/session-writes.json

   \`autocommit\` is the SHA of commit (a) (may be null when the session wrote nothing into the vault), \`paths\` are its worktree-relative files. Use \`--mixed\` (NOT \`--soft\`): it rebuilds the index from the integration base while leaving your working tree — your final content — untouched, so commit (a) can be staged without stealing your own edits.

   Rebuild the history on top of the integration base so commit (a) and commit (b) sit directly on \`origin/main\` (or on the local \`main\` when no origin exists), never squashed together:

       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 reset --mixed origin/main

   Stage commit (a) from the recorded autocommit SHA, so it keeps the session's original bytes even if you edited one of those files later, then commit it. \`git reset <commit> -- <paths>\` writes only the INDEX — it leaves your working tree (your final content) intact, which is what keeps your own edit of a session-written file available for commit (b):

       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 reset -q <autocommit> -- <paths from the manifest...>
       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 commit -m "autocommit: <n> files written by session <label>"

   Skip both commands when the manifest's \`autocommit\` is null. Then commit (b), which owns everything you edited — including your own edit layered on top of a session-written file:

       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 add -A -- . ":(exclude).napkin/distill/"
       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 commit -m "distill: <one-line summary of what you captured>"

   Skip (b) when nothing is staged. Never fold (a) into (b) and never amend one into the other: two commits, always.

   Then push both commits from the worktree:

       git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 push origin HEAD:main

   Never use \`--force\` or \`--force-with-lease\`. If the push is rejected as non-fast-forward because origin moved, retry at most 3 total push attempts: fetch \`origin/main\`, merge it into the distill branch in the worktree, resolve conflicts with the union rule, repeat this step's rebuild (reset --mixed, commit (a) from the autocommit, commit (b)), and push again. If the third attempt is rejected, stop — do not loop indefinitely and do not mutate \`/home/user/Goldmine\` to recover.

   If no origin remote is configured, build the same two commits on top of the local \`main\` (\`git -C /home/user/.cache/napkin-distill/a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8/abc123-1700000000 reset --mixed main\` above, then (a) and (b)), and land them in the main vault without rewriting history:

       git -C /home/user/Goldmine checkout main
       git -C /home/user/Goldmine merge --ff-only distill/abc123-1700000000

   The fast-forward keeps both commits and their messages intact; never \`--squash\` them together.

9. Final integration rule. With origin configured, success means \`origin/main\` contains both commits: the session autocommit (a) and your distill commit (b). Do not use \`/home/user/Goldmine\` as an integration scratchpad and do not run \`git pull\` there; the vault's own sync fast-forwards the shared checkout after the push lands. Without origin, success means both commits are on the local \`main\` in \`/home/user/Goldmine\`. NEVER use \`--force\` or \`--force-with-lease\`. If push fails for a reason other than a retryable non-fast-forward rejection, stop — the wrapper will surface the failure and the dangling commit to the user.

Report progress concisely as you go. Stop when steps 1-9 are complete.
"
